      "claim": "Blocking adenosine receptors reduces sleep pressure, making it harder to fall asleep.",
      "evidence": "Sleep‑science literature shows accumulated adenosine drives sleep onset; antagonism delays this process.",
      "weight": 0.10,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Children have lower body mass and immature caffeine metabolism, so a given dose yields higher plasma caffeine levels than in adults.",
      "evidence": "American Academy of Pediatrics guidelines note higher caffeine concentrations and prolonged half‑life in pediatric patients.",
      "weight": 0.10,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Higher caffeine levels in children lead to greater sleep disruption compared with adults.",
      "evidence": "Pediatric studies report increased sleep latency and reduced total sleep time after caffeine ingestion in children and adolescents.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [2, 3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Large‑scale surveys and clinical trials find a statistically significant association between caffeine intake and difficulty falling asleep or insomnia in youths.",
      "evidence": "AAP 2020 survey of >10,000 U.S. children; randomized crossover trials showing dose‑dependent sleep‑latency increase.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [4]
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "claim": "Systematic reviews of longitudinal growth studies find no causal link between typical coffee consumption and reduced linear growth in children.",
      "evidence": "Meta‑analysis of 12 cohort studies (e.g., WHO Nutrition Review 2019) shows height outcomes are unrelated to caffeine intake after adjusting for nutrition and genetics.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 7,
      "claim": "Therefore, the most direct and well‑supported consequence of excessive coffee consumption in kids is sleep disturbance, not growth impairment.",
